{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>These are the journeys of the children of Israel.</b> Since Moses said above to the children of Gad and the children of Reuben: ״וַיְנִיעֵם בַּמִּדְבָּר אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה״, one might have thought that they were wandering and moving constantly like one lying at the head of a rope — they did not rest and did not settle the entire forty years. Therefore he said 'These are the journeys' — to tell you that during the forty years He did not move them except forty-two times. And through this is resolved what is stated, ״אֵלֶּה מַסְעֵי״, and he did not say ״אֵלֶּה הַחֲנִיּוֹת אֲשֶׁר חָנוּ״, in the manner of what is stated ״וַיִּסְעוּ וַיַּחֲנוּ״; rather, he comes to explain the statement ״וַיְנִיעֵם בַּמִּדְבָּר״, for the movement — that is the journeys. And as for the reason to recount all the journeys, Rashi wrote: to make known the kindnesses of the Place, etc. And the Rav the Moreh gave a different reason. And likewise the Mahar'i'a gave several reasons, and there is no need to elaborate on them."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:46.781147042Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}